12.2  Locales: why sorting, dates and numbers change

TL;DR
  - A locale bundles cultural rules: how bytes become characters (LC_CTYPE),
    sort order (LC_COLLATE), number and date formats, message language. Set by
    LANG, overridden by LC_*, all overridden by LC_ALL.
  - The same command can give different results in another locale: sort order,
    3,14 vs 3.14, month names.
  - In scripts, pin it: LC_ALL=C for byte order and plain ASCII rules, or
    C.UTF-8 to keep UTF-8.

      locale                       # current settings
      locale -a                    # locales installed here
      LC_ALL=C sort file.txt       # sort by bytes, the same everywhere

On your machine
  Session   LANG=en_US.UTF-8
  Text      read as UTF-8 (en_US.UTF-8)
  System    LANG=fr_FR.UTF-8 for new sessions
  Installed C.utf8, and those in locale-archive

Pitfalls
  - printf '%.2f' 3.14 fails in a French locale ("invalid number"): scripts that
    parse or print numbers should set LC_NUMERIC=C.
  - "setlocale: cannot change locale" (often over SSH): your terminal sends a
    LANG the server hasn't generated. Generate it there (locale-gen) or set
    LANG=C.UTF-8.
  - [a-z] can match accented or uppercase letters, depending on the locale and
    the tool's version; [[:lower:]] or LC_ALL=C say what you mean.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why does sort order depend on the locale?

    Human sorting rules differ: ignoring case and accents at first, then
    breaking ties; Swedish puts ä after z, German next to a. The C locale skips
    all that and compares bytes, which is fast and the same everywhere, but puts
    Zebra before apple. Two programs sorting in different locales can disagree,
    which breaks tools like join and comm that expect sorted input.

  ▾ Where do locales come from?

    Locale definitions are compiled into binary files, one per locale or all in
    /usr/lib/locale/locale-archive. Debian and Arch compile only those listed in
    /etc/locale.gen; Fedora ships them in language packs. C.UTF-8 is built into
    recent glibc and always available.

Related
  Commands  locale, localectl, sort
  Paths     /etc/locale.conf, /etc/default/locale, /etc/locale.gen
  See also  idk 12.1 Bytes and characters: ASCII, Unicode and UTF-8
  See also  idk 27.1 Where environment variables come from
  See also  idk 28.9 sort, uniq and cut
